<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-Driven
<br class="ltx_break">Kernel Generation for Edge Backends</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuhua Zheng  Xueyi Chen  Xinze Zou  Xin Li  Haoji Hu  Huan Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><a href="mailto:yuhuazheng@zju.edu.cn,haoji_hu@zju.edu.cn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">{yuhuazheng,haoji_hu}@zju.edu.cn</a></a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Westlake University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Zhejiang University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:mailto:wanghuan@westlake.edu.cnwanghuan@westlake.edu.cnhttps://pillor9.github.io/EdgeKernelBench/">mailto:wanghuan@westlake.edu.cnwanghuan@westlake.edu.cnhttps://pillor9.github.io/EdgeKernelBench/</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent works show that LLMs can generate kernels with strong performance on server GPUs. Edge deployment presents distinct challenges because the hardware is heterogeneous, the software stacks are fragmented, and the resource budgets are tight. LLM kernel generation across diverse edge backends has yet to be studied systematically. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EdgeKernelBench</span>, which combines a unified IR deployment framework with a hierarchical benchmark for edge workloads. The unified IR captures reusable computation, scheduling, and memory decisions and connects to each target backend through a compiler interface. The benchmark spans progressively larger program scopes, from individual operators to compact model graphs, across representative edge application domains. We instantiate the design with TVM as a reference compiler and validate it across heterogeneous edge backends. With a single generation attempt, the best model achieves 64.5% Correct@1 but only 25.5% <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. This gap shows that functional correctness does not consistently translate into performance gains. We therefore introduce the <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layered Edge Kernel Agent (LEKA)</span>, which organizes optimization through a layered IR. On the agent evaluation subset, LEKA achieves 43.5% <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, compared with 13.0% for direct generation and 26.1% for Claude Code. LEKA and Claude Code use the same base model and optimization budget. Together, EdgeKernelBench and LEKA support unified evaluation and layered optimization of kernels generated by LLMs across edge backends.</p>
  
</div>
