<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Growing demand for low latency, reduced network reliance, and local data processing drives model deployment on edge devices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Yet rapidly evolving models must run across diverse runtimes and hardware backends. Deployment systems continue improving inference efficiency across platforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nachin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Still, new operators and backends often require custom kernels. The need to manually implement, validate, and maintain high-performance operators remains a persistent bottleneck in edge deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models can now generate executable code from natural language descriptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Recent works apply this capability to high-performance kernel generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. However, most of this work still targets server GPUs. There are three major problems that prevent these methods from being used to generate kernels for edge devices, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.24648v1/introduction.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/220;" width="548" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span> <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Server and edge kernel generation follow different optimization regimes.</span> Server methods usually emphasize execution speed within relatively uniform software stacks and generous resource budgets. Edge generation spans fragmented hardware and software ecosystems. It must also balance performance, memory use, and deployment feasibility under tight resource limits.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Edge hardware and software ecosystems are highly fragmented.</span> Server kernel generation usually targets a small set of mainstream accelerators supported by mature software stacks. Edge devices span heterogeneous processors, vendor runtimes, and deployment engines. Generating native programs directly ties reusable computation and scheduling knowledge to the languages, interfaces, and legality rules of individual backends. Supporting a new backend therefore requires the model to adapt to another programming and deployment environment, which limits transfer across devices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Edge kernel generation has a different optimization objective from server kernel generation.</span> Server kernels often run on accelerators with ample dedicated memory, allowing optimization to focus primarily on execution speed. Edge workloads share compute and memory with the rest of the system, and memory capacity can determine whether a model is deployable. Edge kernel generation must therefore optimize performance and memory jointly within a fixed resource budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Existing benchmarks do not fully capture the requirements of edge kernel generation.</span> KernelBench and related studies mainly target server GPUs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, while MobileKernelBench extends evaluation to mobile devices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Its public tasks consist mainly of individual operators and their attribute variants. Such tasks assess basic code generation, but not cross-operator optimization or graph-level resource planning in edge deployments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address these problems, we introduce <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EdgeKernelBench</span>. It combines a unified deployment framework with a hierarchical benchmark for edge workloads. The LLM does not produce a separate native program for each backend. The LLM expresses optimization choices for the target device in a unified intermediate representation, or IR. A deterministic backend adapter then lowers the program to the target backend in stages. This separation isolates reusable computation and knowledge from interfaces that are specific to each backend. EdgeKernelBench also places scheduling and memory decisions in the same optimization space. The model can reuse optimization knowledge across backends while balancing execution performance and memory cost under deployment constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">All EdgeKernelBench tasks are PyTorch reference programs with traceable provenance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paszke et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. The tasks are organized along two orthogonal axes, compositional complexity and application domain. The complexity axis contains three levels named <em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Basic Compute Patterns</em>, <em id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Composed Operator Graphs</em>, and <em id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Compact Model Graphs</em>. The application domains cover Mobile and Personal Intelligence, Embodied Robotics, and Smart AIoT. Separating these axes enables targeted evaluation of optimization capabilities across edge deployment settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To test the feasibility of this design, we instantiate EdgeKernelBench with TVM as the reference compiler stack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. We evaluate five LLMs on 650 task instances across CPU/LLVM, GPU/CUDA, and GPU/OpenCL execution paths. The results show that a consistent workflow for generation, validation, and measurement runs across heterogeneous backends. The hierarchical benchmark also reveals how the model capability changes as the program scope grows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The experiments also expose the limits of one-shot generation. Across 650 task instances, the strongest model achieves 73.5% Compile@1 and 64.5% Correct@1, but only 25.5% <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. At Level 3, the highest <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@1.0</annotation></semantics></math> scores on the three backends fall further to values between 0.0% and 4.0%. As the program scope grows, one-shot generation struggles to coordinate increasingly coupled decisions across representation levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">To address this limitation, we further introduce <span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LEKA</span>, the <span id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>ayered <span id="S1.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>dge <span id="S1.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">K</span>ernel <span id="S1.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>gent. LEKA decomposes coupled cross-level decisions along the IR hierarchy, allowing each agent to focus on one representation while preserving coordination across levels. On a randomly sampled 69-instance subset for agent evaluation, LEKA raises Correct@1 from 42.0% to 100.0% and <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@1.0</annotation></semantics></math> from 13.0% to 43.5% relative to one-shot generation. With the same base model, task contract, and wall-clock budget, LEKA raises <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@1.0</annotation></semantics></math> from 26.1% for the pure Claude Code baseline to 43.5%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Our work makes three contributions.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce EdgeKernelBench, which combines a unified IR deployment framework with a hierarchical benchmark for edge workloads. It provides a common basis for studying LLM kernel generation and optimization on edge devices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce LEKA, a general agent architecture for edge kernel generation and optimization. LEKA organizes optimization through a layered IR to reduce the complexity of decisions across representation levels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct systematic experiments on a range of representative edge hardware backends. The evaluation measures the edge kernel generation capabilities of leading LLMs and examines their main failure modes. It also tests the optimization gains from LEKA.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Kernel generation benchmarks.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Benchmarks have spread from server GPUs to NPUs, TPUs, and multi-vendor accelerators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, but the hardware stays largely server-class, the tasks stay server-oriented, and the artifact stays native code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. One benchmark reaches mobile devices, and it covers mainly single operators and their attribute variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Native code leaves the model to meet each backend’s language and legality rules itself, so correctness collapses on hardware it has rarely seen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. These benchmarks mostly share the KernelBench protocol, which organizes <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">PyTorch</span> reference programs by scope and scores generated kernels on correctness and speedup over a software baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. EdgeKernelBench keeps the protocol but evaluates on heterogeneous edge backends, draws its tasks from edge applications, spans single operators to compact model graphs, and replaces native code with one IR shared by all backends.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Compilers and portable kernel representations.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deployment stacks fix their optimization knowledge in expert-written kernels or schedule rules, whereas LLM generators hold such knowledge but emit native code and forgo the portability a compiler provides. Inference engines ship a hand-written kernel for each operator and backend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">David et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA, 2026</a>)</cite>. Tensor compilers lower one program through several IR levels to many backends <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lattner et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and search a rule-defined schedule space with cost models and hundreds to thousands of on-device measurements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Neither can reach a transformation outside its encoding, such as an algebraic rewrite or an unforeseen fusion boundary. EdgeKernelBench puts the LLM’s computation, scheduling, and memory decisions in a unified IR that a deterministic backend adapter lowers to each backend.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM-driven kernel optimization.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Methods beyond one-shot generation differ in where they spend compute but most treat one native program as the unit of optimization. Training-time methods fine-tune a model with execution rewards until it specializes in one kernel language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baronio et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Inference-time methods keep the model fixed and iterate through agent roles with profiler feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, evolutionary search over a candidate lineage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, repair loops inside a mobile framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, or a generate-test-refine loop over a kernel abstraction layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saba et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. That unit entangles algorithm, fusion, thread-mapping, and instruction choices, so a reward scores the whole program and a profile points at a slow kernel, not at the decision level behind it. LEKA gives each IR level its own agent and routes every measurement to the level that owns the decision rather than to the whole program.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>EdgeKernelBench</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.24648v1/benchmark.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/236;" width="548" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of EdgeKernelBench.</span> <span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> We curate workloads relevant to edge deployment as PyTorch tasks and organize them by compositional complexity and application domain. <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> The LLM uses a layered generation interface to express decisions about graphs, scheduling, and memory. A concrete compiler implementation maps these decisions to execution paths. <span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span> Each candidate is built, checked against a PyTorch reference program, and timed against a paired baseline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Benchmark Construction and Organization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark construction.</span> Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 EdgeKernelBench ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a) shows the three-stage construction of EdgeKernelBench. We first select basic computations, model modules, and operator compositions representative of practical edge inference. We draw these tasks from widely used open-source edge models, operator libraries, and kernel repositories. Then, we convert dynamic computation into static form and give every task a common PyTorch interface. This step also separates the core computational semantics from the workload configuration used by a specific application. Finally, we manually inspect each graph for structural consistency and intended semantics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization along two dimensions.</span> EdgeKernelBench contains 331 canonical tasks organized along two orthogonal dimensions. The first dimension captures compositional complexity. <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Level 1 Basic Compute Patterns</em> contains 128 basic operators or core computation patterns. It tests whether an LLM can preserve local semantics, produce a legal implementation, and make basic scheduling decisions for the target. <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Level 2 Composed Operator Graphs</em> contains 162 fused computation graphs or model modules. It tests whether the model can coordinate dependent operators, intermediate results, and fusion choices beyond a single operator. <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Level 3 Compact Model Graphs</em> contains 41 complex modules or compact model graphs. It evaluates execution planning and correctness over longer dependency chains, branches, and larger program scopes. The second dimension groups tasks by edge application. It includes Mobile and Personal Intelligence with 204 instances, Smart AIoT with 228, and Embodied Robotics with 218. Each domain spans all three complexity levels. Every task corresponds to a meaningful model structure from an edge application. Correctness and performance gains on these tasks therefore indicate potential value for practical edge deployment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.24648v1/agent.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/247;" width="548" height="247" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of LEKA.</span> LEKA organizes layer agents along progressive IR lowering. Their scope narrows from the global program structure to implementation details for a specific target. Compiler conversions preserve prior optimizations while opening search spaces at lower levels. The candidate tree stores IR candidates at different levels and records how they were derived. Hardware feedback guides subsequent search at the relevant level.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Unified Generation and Evaluation Framework</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shared representation.</span> Edge deployment spans fragmented software stacks and must meet strict resource limits. Program generation must therefore accommodate different backends while balancing execution performance against memory use. EdgeKernelBench separates the shared task and evaluation contract from the deployment implementation for each backend. Given a PyTorch reference program and a description of the target device, the LLM expresses its optimization decisions in <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Unified Kernel IR</em>. The IR places computation, scheduling, and memory in a shared optimization space while preserving target-specific choices. Backend adapters lower the shared IR into target-specific executable programs. This separation makes optimization intent reusable across edge devices and software stacks. New backends can join the same generation and evaluation workflow through dedicated adapters without changing the shared task contract or evaluation protocol.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unified evaluation pipeline.</span> EdgeKernelBench provides a unified evaluation of LLM-generated edge kernels across heterogeneous backends. The protocol proceeds through candidate construction, functional validation, and performance measurement. These stages reveal deployability and locate failures throughout the process. The evaluation characterizes each candidate by its compilation success, functional correctness, and execution performance. Its unified structure makes the protocol broadly applicable across diverse edge hardware, compiler stacks, and runtime environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Layered Edge Kernel Agent (LEKA)</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EdgeKernelBench provides a unified generation and evaluation interface, yet one LLM call must coordinate decisions across several abstraction levels. For complex tasks, a single agent must retain a global view of the computation graph while reasoning about implementation details at lower levels within a limited context. As program scope grows, this context burden constrains optimization and makes execution feedback harder to attribute. We therefore introduce the <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>ayered <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>dge <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">K</span>ernel <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>gent, or <span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LEKA</span>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Benchmark Construction and Organization ‣ 3 EdgeKernelBench ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, LEKA decomposes optimization by IR level. A shared candidate tree links decisions across levels and gives each layer agent a focused view of the optimization space exposed at its IR level, while hardware feedback drives subsequent iterations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Hierarchical IR Optimization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agents aligned with representation levels.</span> Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> denote a kernel task, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> the target device, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of legal program states at IR level <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. Agent <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> optimizes the representation at level <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> within the semantic boundary and edit scope defined by contract <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">At candidate version <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, LEKA maintains a candidate tree <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{t}=(V_{t},F_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{t}=(V_{t},F_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> contains all program states generated so far, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the transitions produced by within-level optimization or cross-level lowering, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\ell,t}\in V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\ell,t}\in V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the current candidate node at IR level <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. Projection <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\ell,h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\ell,h}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the search history and measurement evidence relevant to the current level and target device. The hardware-sensing agent profiles the candidate on the target device and returns the detected bottlenecks to the current level in a report <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write optimization within a level and conversion between levels as</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{\ell,t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{\ell,t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=A_{\ell}\!\left(x_{\ell,t}\mid q,h,C_{\ell},\Pi_{\ell,h}(\mathcal{T}_{t},v_{\ell,t}),r_{t}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=A_{\ell}\!\left(x_{\ell,t}\mid q,h,C_{\ell},\Pi_{\ell,h}(\mathcal{T}_{t},v_{\ell,t}),r_{t}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{\ell+1,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{\ell+1,t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Lambda_{\ell\rightarrow\ell+1}\!\left(x_{\ell,t}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Lambda_{\ell\rightarrow\ell+1}\!\left(x_{\ell,t}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The compiler supplies <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\ell\rightarrow\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\ell\rightarrow\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which preserves semantics, performs required legalization and interface conversion, and carries existing IR optimizations forward. It does not search for candidates, leaving performance decisions to the layer agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Progressive lowering and focused context.</span> LEKA follows progressive lowering across multiple IR levels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lattner et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. As lowering proceeds, decisions made at upper levels gradually become fixed while finer optimization spaces open. This order lets each decision be optimized at the IR level that expresses it most directly. The scope of each agent narrows in parallel from the full program to local implementation details. Compared with a single agent that must retain both global program structure and low-level details in the same context, LEKA decomposes a complex task into optimization steps aligned with individual IR levels. This decomposition reduces the context required by each call and makes execution feedback easier to attribute to the relevant level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shared abstractions and target specialization.</span> LEKA first searches EdgeKernelBench’s backend-shared IR layers, each covering optimization at a different program granularity. During target-specific lowering, the adapter retains high-level optimizations and exposes more hardware-coupled choices to lower-level agents. This separation allows LEKA to transfer high-level optimizations to new backends and focus the remaining adaptation search on target-specific implementation details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Candidate Search Guided by Evidence</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Candidate search with lineage tracking.</span> LEKA organizes its feedback loop as a lineage search over the candidate tree introduced above <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. With every optimization attempt, LEKA adds a new state while retaining earlier candidates and their validation results. The orange path in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Benchmark Construction and Organization ‣ 3 EdgeKernelBench ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> marks the active optimization trajectory, while the remaining branches preserve alternatives for comparison, rollback, or continued search. Each layer agent expands candidates within its assigned optimization space, and the shared tree connects these local searches across IR levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feedback from hardware evidence.</span> For each executable candidate <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the hardware-sensing agent runs the evaluator on the target device and collects detailed feedback. It uses these measurements to identify the level <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that needs further optimization and writes a report <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for the candidate. The selected layer agent uses the report attached to the node to generate the next candidate, forming a layered iteration driven by device measurements.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation environment.</span> We instantiate EdgeKernelBench and LEKA with TVM 0.23.0<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://tvm.apache.org/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://tvm.apache.org/</a></span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, chosen for its mature open-source ecosystem and established use in practice. Through a modified TVM integration, EdgeKernelBench manages task construction, device descriptions, and the optimization workflow. LEKA optimizes computation graphs in Relax and computation and scheduling in TIR. It refines implementations in CUDA C <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA, 2024</a>)</cite>, OpenCL C <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khronos Group, n.d.</a>)</cite>, or LLVM IR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lattner and Adve, 2004</a>)</cite>. All optimization decisions come from the LLM or agent. TVM handles representation conversion, target code generation, and runtime support.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate three devices to cover different edge hardware and software ecosystems. CPU/LLVM runs on an iQOO Neo9S Pro+ with a Snapdragon 8 Gen 3 SoC. The GPU/CUDA device is an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin 64GB. For GPU/OpenCL, we use a Firefly ROC-RK3588S-PC with a Mali-G610 GPU. We evaluate compilation, correctness, and performance on each device.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> We report compilation success rate (CSR, Compile@1), functional correctness rate (FCR, Correct@1), <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@p</annotation></semantics></math>, and geometric mean speedup (GM). CSR is the fraction of tasks whose generated programs compile successfully. FCR additionally requires all output checks to pass. <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@p</annotation></semantics></math> is the fraction of tasks with correct programs, valid timings, and a median paired speedup above <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> over the baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. GM is the geometric mean of speedups over the specified baseline for valid programs on the same task set, including slower results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation tasks and models.</span> Following the EdgeKernelBench configuration, CPU/LLVM uses tasks from Mobile and Personal Intelligence, GPU/CUDA from Embodied Robotics, and GPU/OpenCL from Smart AIoT. We adapt input shapes to each scenario and retain all three complexity levels. Our one-shot evaluation covers representative proprietary models, including GPT-5.6-sol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026</a>)</cite>, Gemini-3.7-Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google, 2026</a>)</cite>, and Claude Sonnet 5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026</a>)</cite>. We also evaluate the open-weight models DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813 (abbreviated as DeepSeek-V4-Pro) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2026</a>)</cite> and GLM-5.3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Z.ai, 2026b</a>)</cite>. Given time constraints, we use stratified random sampling by complexity level to select 69 task instances across the three backends for LEKA evaluation. LEKA and the pure Claude Code baseline both use GLM-5.3-Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Z.ai, 2026a</a>)</cite> as the base model. They share the same task contracts, evaluator, and time budgets.</p>
</div>
