<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Baseline Evaluation</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Compiler-Baseline Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the TVM compilation pipeline tuned with MetaSchedule as the baseline. We evaluate compilation success, functional correctness, and execution performance under one-shot generation. Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows how generation quality varies with task complexity, hardware backend, and model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across task levels, CSR, FCR, and F@1 generally decline as tasks expand from single operators to operator compositions and multi-module computation graphs. Across the three backends, the highest F@1 falls from 51.0% at Level 1 to 16.2% at Level 2 and 4.0% at Level 3. More complex tasks introduce longer semantic dependency chains. They also require models to coordinate computation and storage across a larger program context. These demands make it harder to maintain program consistency and optimize across operators. As a result, generation capabilities on basic operators do not readily extend to complex computation graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Hardware backends also affect generation quality, with generally higher FCR on CPU than on GPU. At Level 1, the highest FCR reaches 94.5% on CPU, compared with 79.6% on CUDA and 73.5% on OpenCL. Thread partitioning and parallel mapping make valid GPU implementations harder to generate. Across the two GPU configurations, CUDA achieves higher F@1 overall. The advantage is most consistent at Level 1, where every model outperforms its OpenCL result. We hypothesize that this partly reflects greater exposure to high-quality CUDA implementations during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Models also differ in their strengths. Among proprietary models, GPT maintains relatively high F@1 across multiple backends. Gemini performs well on complex tasks, achieving the highest Level 3 FCR on all three backends. Among open-weight models, DeepSeek achieves strong correctness on CPU, reaching 75.3% FCR at Level 2. Its corresponding F@1 is only 3.2%, compared with 15.1% for GPT. Open-weight models can therefore exceed proprietary models in correctness under some configurations, while still trailing in execution performance. Model strengths in program correctness do not necessarily carry over to performance optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2 </span>Production-Inference-Engine Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare one-shot LLM-generated programs with two production inference engines, MNN 3.3.0<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/alibaba/MNN" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/alibaba/MNN</a></span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and TensorRT 10.3.0<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/tensorrt" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://developer.nvidia.com/tensorrt</a></span></span></span>. Given operator and backend limitations, each comparison uses a fixed task subset that the engine supports and executes correctly. We measure generated programs and their corresponding engine implementations on the same target device.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.24648v1/fastp.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/165;" width="548" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance of one-shot LLM-generated programs relative to MNN and TensorRT.</span> The horizontal axis shows the speedup threshold <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and the vertical axis reports <math id="S5.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fast}@p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fast</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fast}@p</annotation></semantics></math> on the fixed task subsets. At <math id="S5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the score is the fraction of tasks with correct outputs and valid timings.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2.2 Production-Inference-Engine Results ‣ 5.2 Baseline Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that programs outperforming MNN are concentrated at Level 1. F@1 peaks at 50.7% on CPU and 31.6% on GPU. The maxima at Levels 2 and 3 fall to 14.9% and 5.3%, respectively. As the speedup threshold increases, curves for complex tasks rapidly approach zero, with large speedups confined to a few tasks. Speedups also differ between CPU and GPU. These differences may reflect how effectively generated programs use CPU vectorization and GPU parallelism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Results against TensorRT show a similar pattern across task levels. The highest F@1 is 43.2% at Level 1 and falls to 5.3% at Level 2. No Level 3 candidate outperforms TensorRT. The Level 1 curves also decline sharply once the speedup threshold exceeds 1. At a threshold of 2, the highest task fraction is only 7.4%. One-shot generation can therefore outperform TensorRT on some basic operators, but large speedups remain limited to a small fraction of tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.3 </span>Error Analysis</h4>

<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:33%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.24648v1/error_distribution.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:187/209;" width="187" height="209"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure distribution.</span> Percentages pool failures across all models and backends.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We group failures into six categories. <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Syntax/API errors</span> cover invalid syntax and incorrect interface use. <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shape/type errors</span> involve incompatible tensor dimensions or data types. <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory errors</span> concern invalid buffer allocation or access. <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel errors</span> violate thread mapping or synchronization constraints. <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness errors</span> indicate output mismatches or consistency violations. <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Other</span> includes timeouts and unresolved compiler, execution, or verification exceptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2.3 Error Analysis ‣ 5.2 Baseline Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> pools failures across all models and backends. Memory and syntax/API errors account for 34.0% and 25.4% of failures, respectively. Most memory errors concern allocation legality, not insufficient capacity. This pattern suggests limited understanding of buffer scope and storage rules in the target representation. Correctness and parallel errors contribute another 18.9% and 12.0%. These failures expose weaknesses in preserving computational semantics and reasoning about dependencies across parallel threads. Overall, these failures reveal limitations in program construction and semantic preservation, informing future edge kernel generation systems.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.24648v1/results.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/124;" width="548" height="124"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LEKA results and layered optimization.</span> (a) FCR and (b) F@1 over TVM on 23 tasks per backend. (c) GM relative to pure Claude Code. (d) Four candidates for SqueezeNet1.1, with candidate-evaluation speedups relative to the initial implementation.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>LEKA-Guided Optimization</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.1 </span>Results against One-Shot Generation and Claude Code</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2.3 Error Analysis ‣ 5.2 Baseline Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(a,b,c) compares LEKA with one-shot generation and pure Claude Code across 69 task instances. Relative to one-shot GLM-5.3-Flash, LEKA raises CSR from 60.9% to 100.0% and FCR from 42.0% to 100.0%. F@1 over TVM increases from 13.0% to 43.5%. These final-program scores show the benefit of iterative generation guided by device feedback.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The agent comparison uses the same base model, task contracts, evaluator, and assigned task-level time budgets. LEKA raises F@1 from 26.1% for Claude Code to 43.5%, with an overall GM of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.8\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.8\times</annotation></semantics></math> relative to Claude Code. Across all three backends, GM is higher at Level 2 than at Level 1, with pooled GM rising from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.4\times</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4.7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.7\times</annotation></semantics></math>. This pattern may reflect LEKA’s layered design and the focused scope of each agent. As more operators are composed, optimization requires longer contexts and involves more interdependent decisions. LEKA distributes these decisions across representation levels, allowing each agent to focus on optimization within its own level. This reduces the burden of handling cross-level details and may explain the larger relative gains at Level 2. At Level 3, GM declines to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.9\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.9\times</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly reflecting the base model’s limited reasoning and optimization capabilities on larger graphs with longer dependency chains. Overall, layered optimization helps manage task complexity, but its gains remain constrained by the base model’s capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.2 </span>Optimization Case Study</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SqueezeNet1.1 case on RK3588 illustrates how LEKA’s agents coordinate cross-level optimization (Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2.3 Error Analysis ‣ 5.2 Baseline Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(d)). The Relax agent adopts NHWC layout and rewrites <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> convolutions as matrix multiplications, exposing opportunities for lower-level optimization. Building on this graph, the TIR agent uses shared-memory tiling to reuse convolution inputs, increasing the recorded speedup from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.3\times</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.7\times</annotation></semantics></math>. The OpenCL agent applies register blocking to the matrix multiplications introduced in Relax while preserving the TIR-optimized convolutions. This refinement reduces repeated input loads and accumulates in registers, bringing speedup over the initial implementation to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5\times</annotation></semantics></math>. These cumulative gains illustrate how LEKA combines focused reasoning with coordinated optimization across layers. Layered decomposition reduces the reasoning burden of complex optimization, while agent cooperation lets complementary improvements build on one another.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fragmented hardware and software ecosystems make it harder for LLMs to adapt and optimize kernels for resource-constrained edge devices. We introduce EdgeKernelBench, which combines a unified IR deployment framework with a hierarchical benchmark. It provides a common foundation for kernel generation, deployment, and evaluation across heterogeneous edge backends. We also introduce LEKA, which organizes optimization search across IR levels and uses device feedback to refine candidate programs. Our evaluation shows that model optimization performance varies with program complexity and target hardware. LEKA outperforms the compiler baseline on more tasks than direct generation or a budget-matched general coding agent across the evaluated backends. These findings also offer a perspective on how optimization responsibilities are divided between models and compilers. Lower-level program representations give models more direct control over hardware but introduce more complex constraints and decisions. As models become more capable, an open question is whether this boundary can shift toward lower levels. A related question is how to define layer boundaries and design IRs that help agents reason about optimization choices and constraints. EdgeKernelBench and LEKA provide an experimental foundation for studying this relationship between model capability, program abstraction, and optimization freedom.</p>
</div>
</section>
