<p id="A1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Static shape profiles are organized by application scenario so that a shared computation can be evaluated at different workload scales across edge use cases. In the profile above, <span id="A1.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DIMENSION_0</span> and <span id="A1.SS2.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DIMENSION_1</span> specify tensor dimensions, <span id="A1.SS2.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CONFIGURATION_0</span> sets a workload parameter, and <span id="A1.SS2.p5.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">scenario_note</span> records the application context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Evaluated LLMs and Inference Settings</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813, GLM-5.3, GPT-5.6-sol, Gemini-3.7-Flash, and Claude Sonnet 5 in the one-shot generation experiments. Due to inference budget constraints, both LEKA and the Pure Claude Code baseline use GLM-5.3-Flash for iterative optimization. All models use their providers’ default inference settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Devices and Evaluation Protocols</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Systems and Compiler Configuration</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware platforms.</span> We evaluate three platforms representing CPU/LLVM, GPU/CUDA, and GPU/OpenCL execution. CPU experiments use a Vivo V2403A/iQOO Neo9S Pro+ with a Snapdragon 8 Gen 3 processor running Android 14. CUDA experiments run on an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin 64GB with Jetson Linux and CUDA 12.6. OpenCL experiments use the Mali-G610 GPU on a Firefly ROC-RK3588S-PC platform.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compiler integration.</span> We use TVM 0.23.0 with modifications to connect LLM-generated programs to compilation and deployment. EdgeKernelBench handles task import and program optimization, while TVM provides backend lowering and runtime support. The three execution paths use LLVM for CPU code generation, CUDA for the Jetson GPU, and OpenCL for the RK3588 GPU.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Correctness and Timing Protocol</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We check each generated program against its PyTorch reference using inputs drawn from three random seeds. Correctness requires matching output structures, shapes, and data types, with numerical differences within the task-specific tolerance. Only programs that pass these checks proceed to performance evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our paired evaluation protocol measures candidate and baseline programs in the same device session with identical inputs and matched execution settings. Timing covers one complete execution of the task, with inputs already placed on the target device. Compilation and deployment-time preparation are excluded, while computation that depends on dynamic inputs remains within the timed execution. Candidate and baseline programs use the same timing boundary within each comparison. For comparisons against TensorRT, CUDA events measure device-side latency. GPU measurements use explicit synchronization to account for execution completion.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Both programs are warmed up before repeated measurement, with warm-up settings matched within each backend. For CPU evaluation, we keep worker threads on designated cores and repeat each execution to improve timing consistency. Jetson runs in MAXN mode with its GPU clock verified at approximately 1.3 GHz, while the RK3588 GPU is locked at 1 GHz.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Timing Stability and Paired Measurement</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the robust coefficient of variation (rCV) to assess the stability of repeated measurements. We define a unified stability criterion of rCV no greater than 5% for candidate latency, baseline latency, and paired speedup. For a positive measurement sequence <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, rCV is the scaled median absolute deviation divided by the sample median,</p>
<table id="A2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rCV}(x)=\frac{1.4826\,\operatorname{median}_{i}\!\left|x_{i}-\operatorname{median}(x)\right|}{\operatorname{median}(x)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rCV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1.4826</mn><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>median</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>median</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>median</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rCV}(x)=\frac{1.4826\,\operatorname{median}_{i}\!\left|x_{i}-\operatorname{median}(x)\right|}{\operatorname{median}(x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This measure limits the influence of isolated outliers when estimating relative timing variability.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Paired measurements balance the execution order of candidate and baseline programs to reduce bias from device-state drift. For each retained pair <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{baseline},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>baseline</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{baseline},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\mathrm{candidate},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>candidate</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\mathrm{candidate},i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the baseline and candidate latencies. We compute <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=t_{\mathrm{baseline},i}/t_{\mathrm{candidate},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>baseline</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>candidate</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=t_{\mathrm{baseline},i}/t_{\mathrm{candidate},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and report the median paired speedup. We assess paired stability by computing rCV over <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> alongside the two latency sequences. This checks the repeatability of both individual timings and the relative performance comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Agent Evaluation Tasks and Budgets</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task selection.</span> Iterative agent optimization requires substantially more evaluation time per task than direct generation. For the agent comparison in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 LEKA-Guided Optimization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, we select a subset by stratified random sampling across complexity levels. We sample 23 tasks per backend, comprising 10 Level 1, 9 Level 2, and 4 Level 3 tasks, for 69 task instances in total. This allocation retains all three optimization scopes within the available evaluation budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimization budgets.</span> LEKA and Pure Claude Code receive identical optimization time budgets for each task. The per-task upper limits are 90 minutes for Level 1 and 155 minutes for Levels 2 and 3. These limits bound the time available for iterative generation and evaluation on each task.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance aggregation.</span> Following the metrics used in the main experiments, we report F@1 relative to TVM and GM relative to Pure Claude Code for the final valid programs. F@1 measures the fraction of evaluated tasks with correct implementations and valid timings that outperform the TVM baseline. For Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2.3 Error Analysis ‣ 5.2 Baseline Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(c), GM aggregates the per-task ratios of Pure Claude Code latency to LEKA latency, including tasks where LEKA is slower. A GM above one indicates an overall speed advantage for LEKA on the evaluated task set.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Baseline Construction and Fairness Controls</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>TVM Compiler Baseline</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Budgeted compiler reference.</span> We construct the TVM baseline by importing each PyTorch reference and extracting the optimization tasks for MetaSchedule. Before evaluation, we search and measure candidate schedules on the target hardware using the convergence-calibrated budgets described below. The resulting tuning database stores the schedules and their measured latencies for each workload and target. We build the baseline from the lowest-latency valid schedule found for the matching workload and target within this search budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backend-specific calibration.</span> A benchmark task may yield multiple MetaSchedule tuning tasks, each corresponding to a PrimFunc exposed after graph lowering and fusion. Since convolution, matrix multiplication, reduction, and elementwise programs induce substantially different search spaces, a single fixed trial count would allocate the same cost to tasks with very different scheduling difficulty. We group the extracted tasks into the six classes in Table <a href="#A3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ C.1 TVM Compiler Baseline ‣ Appendix C Baseline Construction and Fairness Controls ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> according to their dominant computation patterns and associated search complexity. For each backend and class, we select representative PrimFuncs with different input shapes, covering smaller and larger computation sizes where available. Each configuration is tuned independently with two random seeds. During calibration, the isolated PrimFunc receives the entire preset ceiling: 1,000 hardware trials on CPU/LLVM and 1,500 on the two GPU backends. All runs use an XGBoost cost model, the gradient task scheduler, and <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">num_trials_per_iter</span>=64.</p>
</div>
