<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence-derived budgets.</span> Let <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{b,r,s}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{b,r,s}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the valid latency measured at hardware trial <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for representative PrimFunc and shape <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, random seed <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and backend <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. The best valid latency observed through trial <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(t)=\min_{1\leq j\leq t}\ell_{b,r,s}(j)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(t)=\min_{1\leq j\leq t}\ell_{b,r,s}(j),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the calibration trial limit and <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(T_{\max})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(T_{\max})</annotation></semantics></math> is the final incumbent. We define the convergence trial as</p>
<table id="A3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}T^{\mathrm{conv}}_{b,r,s}=\min\bigl\{t\ \bigm|\ &amp;\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(t)\leq(1+\epsilon)\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(T_{\max}),\\[-2.84526pt]
&amp;\min_{t\leq j&lt;t+W}\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(j)\geq(1-\epsilon)\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(t),\quad t+W\leq T_{\max}+1\bigr\},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>conv</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>min</mi><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="1.000em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}T^{\mathrm{conv}}_{b,r,s}=\min\bigl\{t\ \bigm|\ &amp;\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(t)\leq(1+\epsilon)\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(T_{\max}),\\[-2.84526pt]
&amp;\min_{t\leq j&lt;t+W}\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(j)\geq(1-\epsilon)\ell^{\star}_{b,r,s}(t),\quad t+W\leq T_{\max}+1\bigr\},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0.02</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=100</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the selected point is already within 2% of the final incumbent and is followed by 100 trials without a further improvement greater than 2%.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.24648v1/tvm_metaschedule_calibration.svg" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/165;" width="548" height="165"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="A3.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MetaSchedule calibration curves.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.1 TVM Compiler Baseline ‣ Appendix C Baseline Construction and Fairness Controls ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> illustrates calibration on three representative workloads, each evaluated with two seeds. Panels (a), (b), and (c) show batched matrix multiplication on CPU/LLVM, GEMM with bias and elementwise multiplication on GPU/CUDA, and GEMM with bias and ReLU on GPU/OpenCL, respectively. Their input shapes are <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(8,48,48)\times(8,48,129)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>48</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>48</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>48</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>129</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(8,48,48)\times(8,48,129)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(6,592)\times(592,296)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6,592</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>592,296</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(6,592)\times(592,296)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,128)\times(128,8)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4,128</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>128</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,128)\times(128,8)</annotation></semantics></math>. Each curve tracks the percentage gap between the best-so-far latency and the final best latency for that seed, with both axes on logarithmic scales. Open markers indicate convergence, the shaded region marks gaps below 2%, and vertical dashed lines indicate the adopted budgets. Zero gaps are displayed at 0.1% to accommodate the logarithmic axis.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For class <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{b,c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{b,c}</annotation></semantics></math> contain all successful representative PrimFunc–shape–seed configurations used on backend <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Its nominal per-PrimFunc budget is</p>
<table id="A3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{b,c}=\max\!\left\{64,64\left\lceil\frac{1.25\max_{(r,s)\in\mathcal{R}_{b,c}}T^{\mathrm{conv}}_{b,r,s}}{64}\right\rceil\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.808em;" width="1.808em"><mi>max</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1.25</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>conv</mi></msubsup></mrow><mn>64</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{b,c}=\max\!\left\{64,64\left\lceil\frac{1.25\max_{(r,s)\in\mathcal{R}_{b,c}}T^{\mathrm{conv}}_{b,r,s}}{64}\right\rceil\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The 25% reserve absorbs variation across shapes and search seeds, while rounding to a multiple of 64 aligns the cap with the hardware-measurement batch. Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.1 TVM Compiler Baseline ‣ Appendix C Baseline Construction and Fairness Controls ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows the C2 representatives that determine the reported budget on each evaluated backend.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark task allocation.</span> For each benchmark task, we set <span id="A3.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_trials_global</span> to the sum of the class budgets assigned to all extracted MetaSchedule tasks and set <span id="A3.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max_trials_per_task</span> to the largest of those budgets, while retaining <span id="A3.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">num_trials_per_iter</span>=64. The gradient task scheduler allocates this total budget adaptively among the extracted tasks, subject to the shared per-task limit. The total search budget therefore reflects both the number and the computational characteristics of the PrimFuncs extracted from a benchmark task.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimization cost.</span> MetaSchedule search repeatedly compiles and measures candidate schedules on the target device. This repeated compilation and hardware measurement adds optimization time as the number of tasks and candidate schedules grows. Direct LLM generation avoids this repeated online schedule search. The generated program is then compiled, validated, and measured. Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that one-shot generation can outperform the tuned TVM baseline on a subset of tasks. These results suggest a route to reducing hardware search costs while producing efficient implementations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fairness controls.</span> TVM and LLM-generated programs follow the correctness and paired measurement protocol in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Devices and Evaluation Protocols ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. We compare freshly measured execution times without applying additional MetaSchedule tuning to the LLM-generated programs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>MNN Inference-Engine Baseline</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fixed inference-engine reference.</span> To compare generated programs with implementations from a mature edge inference engine, we construct a baseline using MNN 3.3.0. Each PyTorch reference program is first exported to ONNX opset 17 and then converted to an MNN model under a common unquantized FP32 numerical contract. We enable MNN’s speed-preferred graph optimization and execute the resulting model through the native target backend. Execution uses the hand-optimized operator implementations maintained within MNN, together with its graph optimizer and backend scheduler. For multi-operator Level 2 and Level 3 tasks, we measure one complete execution of the workload graph; operator partitioning and fusion performed internally by MNN are both treated as valid inference-engine implementations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation coverage.</span> We evaluate MNN on the backends and workloads it supports, with correctness verified against the PyTorch references. The comparison covers 176 CPU/LLVM tasks and 203 GPU/OpenCL tasks. The Level 1, 2, and 3 counts are 73/84/19 for CPU/LLVM and 79/101/23 for GPU/OpenCL. Our MNN evaluation covers CPU and OpenCL execution, while TensorRT provides the inference-engine comparison on CUDA. All LLMs use the same task set within each backend to compute CSR, FCR, and F@1.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Paired performance measurement.</span> We compare MNN with LLM-generated programs using the correctness and paired timing protocol in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Devices and Evaluation Protocols ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>TensorRT Inference-Engine Baseline</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline construction and precision settings.</span> We use TensorRT 10.3.0 to construct the GPU/CUDA inference-engine baseline on the Jetson AGX Orin 64GB. Each PyTorch reference program is exported through ONNX opset 17, after which TensorRT optimizes the graph and builds the engine. TensorRT retains the data types and type conversions defined in each reference program. Quantization, TF32, and automatic precision reduction are disabled. TensorRT performs constant folding, operator fusion, and algorithm selection at the original precision. We use TensorRT’s built-in implementations without adding external custom plugins or handwritten kernels. For Level 2 and Level 3 tasks, we deploy and measure the complete computation graph, allowing TensorRT to choose its internal fusion and execution plan.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task coverage and correctness validation.</span> We evaluate TensorRT on supported tasks that pass the correctness checks described in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Devices and Evaluation Protocols ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. This yields 215 of the 218 GPU/CUDA tasks. Levels 1, 2, and 3 contain 95, 95, and 25 supported tasks, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Paired performance measurement.</span> We compare TensorRT with LLM-generated programs using the correctness and paired timing protocol in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Devices and Evaluation Protocols ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Unified IR and Deployment Framework</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Unified Generation and Evaluation Framework ‣ 3 EdgeKernelBench ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> introduces the shared representation and evaluation pipeline. Here we describe the optimization scope, generation contract, and compiler integration.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Unified IR and Optimization Scope</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unified Kernel IR describes deployment programs at two complementary levels. The graph and invocation level expresses computational dependencies, fusion boundaries, and the flow of intermediate results. The computation and scheduling level specifies loops, data layouts, memory accesses, and parallel mappings. Together, these levels let the model coordinate local kernel changes with the organization of the surrounding program.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Memory decisions span both levels. Fusion and decomposition determine which intermediate tensors are materialized and how long they remain live. Local scheduling determines data reuse and temporary storage within each computation. Exposing these choices allows the model to weigh execution latency against memory use under the target device’s resource constraints. The representation accommodates different parallel strategies and storage organizations for different targets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Generation Contract and Program Construction</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PyTorch reference defines the computation, while input and output specifications and numerical tolerances define the program’s observable behavior. Within this contract, the LLM can reorganize computations, intermediate layouts, fusion boundaries, and execution schedules. These choices preserve the task interface while allowing different implementations of the same computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The framework builds an executable program from the generated artifact and checks its structure and compatibility with the target backend. Numerical validation then verifies that its outputs agree with the PyTorch reference within the specified tolerances.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Compiler Integration and Backend Deployment</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Progressive lowering converts the representation into an executable artifact through compiler transformations. These transformations resolve storage and execution details under the backend’s constraints. The deployment path loads the artifact and binds its entry point to the runtime interface.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our implementation uses TVM’s layered IR, code generation, and runtime support for program construction and device execution. Within this pipeline, EdgeKernelBench provides task and generation interfaces through which LLMs optimize operator implementations, then passes the programs to the evaluator. Backend integration lowers LLM-generated programs from the unified IR to the target backend without introducing additional optimization. The lowered programs follow compilation and runtime paths for CPU/LLVM, GPU/CUDA, or GPU/OpenCL. These components provide backend integration and runtime support for the generated programs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Compiler Instantiation and Hardware Adaptation</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When instantiating EdgeKernelBench with a new compiler, we implement the unified generation interface using that compiler’s IR. The LLM then writes operator implementations and expresses optimization decisions in this representation. Compiler adapters connect the generated programs to the corresponding lowering, code generation, and runtime paths. For new hardware supported by the compiler, we extend the target configuration and deployment adapter to connect to the device runtime. These extensions retain the same task definitions and evaluation criteria.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Failure Analysis and Case Studies</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Backend- and Model-Stratified Failure Analysis</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend Section <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS3" title="5.2.3 Error Analysis ‣ 5.2 Baseline Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2.3</span></a>’s analysis with error definitions and comparisons across models and backends. Figure <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Backend- and Model-Stratified Failure Analysis ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Case Studies ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a> shows the failure composition of one-shot generation for each model and backend.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Syntax/API</span> includes incomplete source responses, invalid program syntax, undefined symbols, unavailable interfaces, and malformed program structure. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory</span> covers illegal buffer-allocation scopes, pointer or address-space declarations, and invalid memory accesses; <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel</span> includes missing thread bindings, invalid launch or work-group mappings, and synchronization patterns rejected by backend legality checks. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shape/type</span> captures violations of tensor rank, broadcasting, reshape, data-type, or vector-width contracts, whereas <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness</span> denotes output-value or consistency mismatches. <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Other</span> covers timeouts and remaining compiler, execution, or verification exceptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<figure id="A5.SS1.p3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:397.5pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.24648v1/failures.svg" id="A5.SS1.p3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/124;" width="548" height="124"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span><span id="A5.SS1.p3.fig1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure composition by model and backend.</span> Each model group contains CPU/LLVM, GPU/CUDA, and GPU/OpenCL subcolumns; rows indicate error categories. Each subcolumn reports the composition of unsuccessful one-shot outcomes and sums to 100.0% before rounding. Zero denotes no observed failures in that category, including CPU/LLVM Parallel entries. All groups share the upper-left color scale; percentages describe failure composition rather than failure rates.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On CPU/LLVM, Syntax/API accounts for 54.0% of GPT-5.6-sol failures, while Memory accounts for 56.2% of Gemini-3.7-Flash failures. This contrast points to different obstacles in constructing valid programs within the same execution environment. The former concerns expressing computations through valid syntax and interfaces, while the latter concerns buffer scope and legal memory access. Memory remains the largest failure category for Gemini-3.7-Flash and Claude Sonnet 5 on both GPU backends, accounting for more than half of their failures in each case. The persistence of this pattern across backends suggests that memory management is a recurring difficulty for these models. These results indicate persistent limitations in LLMs’ memory management capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For GPT-5.6-sol, Parallel is the largest failure category on CUDA at 29.7%, while Syntax/API leads on OpenCL at 30.9%. The CUDA failures point to difficulties in satisfying thread-mapping and synchronization constraints, whereas the OpenCL results place greater emphasis on valid program construction. These contrasting profiles favor backend-specific feedback that identifies which constraints a candidate violates. Numerical correctness also remains a challenge, accounting for 32.5% of DeepSeek-V4-Pro failures on OpenCL. These programs compile and execute but fail to reproduce the reference outputs within the required tolerances. This gap points to limitations in preserving computational semantics when translating reference computations into optimized implementations. Overall, these results reveal limitations in LLMs’ ability to construct valid programs, manage memory and parallel execution, and preserve computational semantics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Optimization Case Studies</h3>

<section id="A5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.2.1 </span>One-shot Generation Case Studies</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We examine two one-shot implementations through generated code and paired measurements following the protocol in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Devices and Evaluation Protocols ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Selective fusion and intermediate storage.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the OpenCL ConvNeXt block, Gemini-3.7-Flash reduces latency from 18.0 ms to 0.6 ms, achieving a <math id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="30.9\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30.9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.9\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over TVM. The implementation uses separate kernels for depthwise convolution and channel transformations to accommodate their different memory access patterns. The convolution kernel reuses spatial neighborhoods in workgroup memory and writes its output in the layout required by the next stage. The second kernel fuses normalization, two linear transformations, activation, and the residual connection. It avoids writing the full expanded hidden activation to global memory. For this task, GPT-5.6-sol generates one kernel that fuses the entire block. Its implementation tiles the hidden channels and repeatedly synchronizes the workgroup between tile computations. It achieves only <math id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0.9\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9\times</annotation></semantics></math> the performance of its corresponding TVM baseline. This comparison suggests that extensive fusion does not necessarily yield better performance. Separating stages with different access patterns can improve data reuse and reduce synchronization overhead, even when it introduces an intermediate tensor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Joint optimization of dequantization and matrix multiplication.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the CPU dequantization and matrix multiplication task, Gemini-3.7-Flash reduces latency from 39.7 ms with MNN to 2.1 ms. The paired speedup is <math id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="19.0\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19.0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.0\times</annotation></semantics></math>. The reference program converts integer weights to floating point, applies zero points and scales, and then performs matrix multiplication. The generated implementation reuses the reference program’s integer weights and per-channel quantization parameters. It moves zero-point correction and scaling after the reduction, reading integer weights directly for floating-point accumulation. This rearrangement avoids creating and storing the entire weight matrix in floating-point form. The implementation parallelizes blocks of output channels and uses vectorized loops to access contiguous weights and accumulate outputs. For the <math id="A5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2048\times 5632" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2048</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5632</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2048\times 5632</annotation></semantics></math> weight matrix, it avoids a 44 MiB floating-point intermediate. The case illustrates how algebraic rearrangement can reduce both intermediate storage and elementwise work within the reduction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">E.2.2 </span>LEKA Optimization Case Studies</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We trace two LEKA runs with GLM-5.3-Flash to examine how optimization proceeds across program layers. The final implementations are compared against the tuned TVM baselines using the evaluation protocol in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Devices and Evaluation Protocols ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">From graph rewrites to device-level refinement.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the CUDA task combining batched matrix multiplication, instance normalization, addition, and multiplication, LEKA achieves a latency of 16.2 <math id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s in formal evaluation. The final implementation achieves a <math id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over the tuned TVM baseline at 19.8 <math id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s. This computation mixes matrix arithmetic and reductions with short elementwise operations, leaving optimization opportunities at several program layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">L0 first fuses the final elementwise operations. It uses the parameter preparation mechanism to move fixed-weight layout conversion and normalization constant preparation outside the repeated inference call. Given the weight layout, L1 fuses matrix multiplication with bias addition and reschedules the matrix computation and normalization reduction. These changes reduce the search-stage latency of this branch from approximately 104.0 <math id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s to 20.0 <math id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s. L2 redistributes work within thread blocks to avoid duplicate weight loads and uses vectorized accesses and register reuse to reduce memory traffic. Warp-level reductions also reduce block-wide synchronization in the normalization kernel from eight barriers to one. The resulting implementation runs in approximately 16.2 <math id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The trajectory reveals dependencies between decisions. The graph layer establishes the parameter layout and computation boundaries that the scheduling layer uses to organize parallel work. Source-level refinement improves memory access and synchronization within it. After an upstream change, LEKA rebuilds the affected downstream implementations and retains the best verified complete program. This preserves compatible improvements as search moves between layers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hardware feedback directs schedule refinement.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the CPU task combining convolution with addition, scaling, sigmoid, and group normalization, LEKA reaches 17.0 ms in formal evaluation. This is a <math id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.6\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.6\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over the TVM baseline at 27.5 ms. Although the graph contains several elementwise and reduction stages, convolution still accounts for approximately 85.0% of the profiled interval after the first effective scheduling pass. This evidence directs further optimization toward data reuse and register blocking in the convolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">L1 constructs a convolution microkernel that computes several output channels and adjacent spatial positions together. It retains accumulations in registers and uses loop unrolling and vectorization to reuse inputs. This optimization reduces the search-stage latency to 19.7 ms. With profiling showing that convolution remains dominant, L1 expands the microtile from eight output channels by four spatial positions to eight channels by eight positions. The wider spatial tile amortizes each weight load over more multiply-accumulate operations while keeping the accumulators within the register budget. This refinement lowers the search-stage latency to approximately 17.0 ms.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Hardware feedback identifies the dominant computation, and L1 refines its loops while preserving the graph and call interfaces. The implementation preserves the L0 graph and achieves its gains through L1 scheduling without further L2 code changes. The case illustrates how the layered design supports sustained optimization within the layer that contains the measured bottleneck.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Generation and Agent Prompt Templates</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section describes the prompts used for direct generation and agent-based optimization. Task subsets, scoring conventions, and agent budgets are specified in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Agent Evaluation Tasks and Budgets ‣ Appendix B Devices and Evaluation Protocols ‣ EdgeKernelBench: Unified LLM-DrivenKernel Generation for Edge Backends" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>One-Shot Generation Prompt Protocol</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We outline the prompt structure and the instructions shared across tasks. The prompt introduces the optimization objective and program requirements, provides device context and one demonstration, and concludes with the target task. We group device capabilities and backend constraints under a single <em id="A6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Device Hardware Profile</em> in the template below. The demonstration illustrates the expected implementation format, while the target task supplies the PyTorch reference and tensor specifications. One-shot generation returns one candidate without iterative evaluation feedback.</p>
</div>
