<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation covers CPU/LLVM, GPU/CUDA, and GPU/OpenCL, but does not yet include specialized accelerators such as NPUs. We evaluate each task on a CPU or a GPU separately, without partitioning its computation between the two for cooperative execution. Such settings involve task allocation and resource sharing across compute units and would broaden the evaluation scope.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.2 </span>Future Work</h3>

<div id="A7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Future work could explore how the organization of the unified IR supports focused agent reasoning and coordinated optimization. Using multilevel compiler infrastructure such as the MLIR-based IREE,<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://iree.dev/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://iree.dev/</a></span></span></span> EdgeKernelBench could expose finer-grained representations for optimization and support different divisions of work among agents. Comparing layer boundaries, context sharing, and cross-layer feedback would help identify how to narrow each agent’s search space while retaining opportunities to jointly optimize computation, memory use, and scheduling.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Extending the evaluation to NPUs would enable research on program optimization under specialized compute and on-chip memory constraints. For CPUs and GPUs within the same device, we could jointly optimize computation partitioning, data transfers, and synchronization for a single task. This would allow us to examine how the benefits of heterogeneous execution vary with workloads and resource budgets, and to explore trade-offs between performance and memory use.</p>
</div>
</section>
